---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'package', label: 'Install the package' },
   { id: 'verify', label: 'Verify with doctor' },
   { id: 'browser', label: 'Browser' },
   { id: 'screen-readers', label: 'VoiceOver and NVDA' },
   { id: 'mcp', label: 'MCP client' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Install"
   summary="Install the a11ied package, check the host with doctor, and add the setup each target needs. The virtual screen reader works right after install. VoiceOver and NVDA need one setup command per machine."
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section class="split panel" id="package">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Package</p>
            <h2>Install the package</h2>
         </div>
         <p>Install globally when you want the <code>a1</code> command in your shell.</p>
         <pre><code>npm install --global a11ied</code></pre>
         <p>Install into a project when you want the TypeScript API or a repository-local CLI.</p>
         <pre><code>npm install a11ied</code></pre>
         <p>
            The package installs two identical binaries, <code>a1</code> and <code>a11ied</code>.
            Every example in these docs uses <code>a1</code>.
         </p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">No install</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            <code>npx -y a11ied mcp</code> starts the MCP server without installing anything. Use
            it in an MCP client config so the client always runs the published version.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="verify">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Verify</p>
         <h2>Check the host with doctor</h2>
      </div>
      <pre><code>a1 doctor</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>✓ Ready for a1 commands on macOS 26.0 (arm64)

Targets
  ✓ macos-voiceover  ready  Ready for real screen reader sessions.
      ✓ AppleScript control enabled in VoiceOver settings
      ✓ AppleScript control confirmed by macOS
      ✓ VoiceOver local preferences exist
      ✓ VoiceOver welcome dialog suppressed
      ✓ Guidepup VoiceOver preferences bundle installed
      ✓ Screen recording available to the current host app
  - windows-nvda     Only available on Windows.
  ✓ virtual-dom      ready  No setup needed.

Browser automation
  ✓ Google Chrome  ready  Used for a1 axe scans.
      Path    /Applications/Google Chrome.app/Contents/MacOS/Google Chrome
      Launch  system install, opened through the "chrome" browser channel

  Policy    system-browser-first: an installed Chrome, Edge, Brave, or Chromium is used before Playwright's Chromium
  Fallback  npx playwright install chromium

Environment
  a11ied  0.1.0
  Node    v24.19.0
  npm     11.17.0
  Host    macOS 26.0 (arm64) [darwin]</code></pre>
      <p>
         The first line is the verdict. When a check fails, it switches to <code>✗</code> with the
         number of steps left, and an <strong>Action items</strong> list with the exact commands
         appears right below it. Targets that only exist on another OS get one dim line.
         <code>a1 doctor --json</code> wraps the same report in the standard envelope, under
         <code>result</code>, with <code>ready</code>, the ordered <code>actions</code>, and each
         target's <code>checks</code>. <code>a1 doctor --strict</code> exits with code 3 while a
         required step is missing, which makes it usable as a CI gate.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="split panel" id="browser">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Browser</p>
            <h2>Give a11ied a Chromium browser</h2>
         </div>
         <p>
            <code>a1 axe</code> loads the page in browser automation. It looks for an installed
            Chrome, Edge, Brave, or Chromium first, then falls back to Playwright's managed
            Chromium.
         </p>
         <p>If <code>doctor</code> detects no browser, install one:</p>
         <pre><code>npx playwright install chromium</code></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">No browser needed</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            <code>a1 wcag</code> reads local data and starts no browser. <code>a1 tree</code> and
            <code>a1 audit</code> need one, since they run the page for real.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="screen-readers">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Real targets</p>
         <h2>Set up VoiceOver or NVDA</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         Guidepup provisions the automation permissions and the screen reader assets each real target
         needs. <code>a1 setup</code> runs its commands for this host, once per machine, and ends
         with the <code>doctor</code> report so you can see every check pass.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 setup</code></pre>
      <p>
         On macOS it runs <code>npx -y @guidepup/setup setup</code>, which turns on VoiceOver's
         AppleScript control and creates the local VoiceOver preferences file, then
         <code>npx -y @guidepup/setup install</code>, which downloads the VoiceOver preferences
         bundle that every session mounts on start. On Windows only the install step runs and it
         downloads the NVDA build. The assets land in Guidepup's cache
         (<code>~/Library/Caches/guidepup</code> on macOS,
         <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\guidepup</code> on Windows). Each Guidepup command keeps the
         terminal while it runs, so accept any permission prompts it shows.
      </p>
      <p>
         Add <code>--skip-setup</code> or <code>--skip-install</code> to run one step only. The
         command exits with code 3 when a step fails or <code>doctor</code> still reports a
         required step. <code>a1 setup --json</code> wraps the step outcomes and the closing
         doctor report in the standard envelope instead of printing progress lines.
      </p>
      <p>
         To run Guidepup by hand instead: <code>install</code> reads the asset list from
         <code>@guidepup/guidepup</code>, so run it from a directory where that package resolves,
         which for a global install is <code>$(npm root -g)/a11ied</code>.
         <code>a1 doctor</code> prints that command with the right <code>cd</code> whenever the
         bundle is missing.
      </p>
      <p>
         Screen recording on macOS needs one more permission. The app that runs <code>a1</code>,
         whether that is Terminal, iTerm, or your editor, needs Screen Recording access in System
         Settings. See <a href="/guides/recording">record a session</a>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="mcp">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">MCP</p>
         <h2>Add the server to an MCP client</h2>
      </div>
      <pre data-label="CONFIG"><code>{`{
  "mcpServers": {
    "a11ied": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "a11ied", "mcp"]
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>
      <p>
         The server speaks stdio and opens no network port. Restart the client after editing the
         config, then ask the agent to call <code>doctor</code> to confirm the connection.
      </p>
      <p>
         Point <code>command</code> at <code>a1</code> with <code>args: ["mcp"]</code> instead if you
         installed globally and want the client to use that copy.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Next</p>
         <h2>Run a test</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         The <a href="/quickstart">quickstart</a> uses the virtual target, so it works as soon as the
         package installs.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
